70 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 56 0 R
/E (Abstract Queen is an open-source electronic cash system designed to enable peer-to-peer digital transactions without relying on a centralized authority. The system implements a blockchain-based ledger using an unspent transaction output \(UTXO\) model, cryptographic digital signatures, transaction identifiers, Merkle roots, and CPU-based Proof-of-Work mining. Transactions are validated independently by participating nodes, including verification of signatures, ownership of referenced UTXOs, transaction amounts, and prevention of double spending. Blocks are independently verified before being added to the local blockchain, allowing nodes to determine validity from the consensus rules they implement rather than trusting the miner that produced a block. Queen is implemented in Python and includes wallet, transaction, mining, blockchain, and peer-to-peer networking components. The project is currently under active development, with development focused on maintaining a simple, transparent, and independently verifiable electronic cash system.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
71 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 56 0 R
/T (1. Introduction)
/E (1. Introduction)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
72 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 56 0 R
/E (Since the introduction of Bitcoin, peer-to-peer electronic cash has demonstrated that digital value can be transferred between participants without requiring a central authority to maintain the ledger. Queen continues this approach by implementing an open-source electronic cash system based on a blockchain, unspent transaction outputs \(UTXO\), cryptographic signatures, and Proof-of-Work. The system is designed so that transactions and blocks are independently validated by participating nodes according to the same consensus rules. Transaction validation includes checking referenced UTXOs, ownership through public-key verification, transaction amounts, duplicate inputs, and digital signatures, while block validation includes verification of the previous chain, Merkle root, transaction identifiers, Proof-of-Work, block rewards, and other consensus conditions. Queen is implemented in Python and includes components for wallets, transactions, blockchain management, mining, and peer-to-peer communication. The project is currently under active development, with its implementation focused on providing a transparent and independently verifiable electronic cash system.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
